{{also|Welcome}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|welcome}}, {{m|enm|wolcume}}, {{m|enm|wulcume}}, {{m|enm|wilcume}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wilcuma}} {{gloss|“a wished-for guest”; compare also {{m|ang|wilcume||welcome!|pos=interjection}}}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*willjakwemō}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wiljakwemô|t=a wished-for arrival or guest}}, possibly from {{m|gem-pro|*wiljakwemaną|t=to be welcome}}, equivalent to {{com|en|will|t1=desire|come|t2=comer, arrival}}. The component ''wil-'' was replaced by ''wel-'' when the sense “guest” of the second component was no longer understood, likely under influence from the adverb {{m|en|well}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|walcome}}, {{cog|fy|wolkom}}, {{cog|nl|welkom}} (earlier ''willecome''), {{cog|de|willkommen}}, {{cog|nds-de|willkamen}}, {{cog|da|velkommen}}, {{cog|nb|velkommen}}, {{cog|nn|velkomen}}, {{m|nn|velkommen}}, {{cog|sv|välkommen}}, {{cog|is|velkominn}}, {{cog|fo|vælkomin}}, and {{cog|fro|wilecome}} (whence {{cog|frm|willecomme|t=welcome}}), from Germanic.

The verb is from {{inh|en|enm|welcomen}}, {{m|enm|wolcumen}}, {{m|enm|wilcumen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wellcumian}}, {{m|ang|wylcumian}}, {{m|ang|wilcumian|t=to welcome, receive gladly}}.

Similar constructions are found in [[Romance language]]s, such as {{ncog|it|benvenuto}}, {{ncog|es|bienvenido}},  {{ncog|fr|bienvenu}}, {{ncog|ca|benvingut}}, {{ncog|pt|bem-vindo}} and {{ncog|ro|bun venit}}, meaning “[may you have fared] well [in] coming [here]”. These do not derive from a Classical Latin root, as no similar construction in Latin is found to exist, but are instead presumed to be the result of a calque from, considering the ruling elite of the Germanic kingdoms which succeeded the {{w|Western Roman Empire}}, a Germanic language into Proto-Romance (Vulgar Latin; see {{cog|la|*bene venūtus}}, and compare {{m|la|perdōnō}} and {{m|la|compāniō}} for similar historical calques).

